original_description_sprint:
To make tracing more useful for error reporting and discovering performance bottlenecks, I will do the following works:

1. The configuration _enable_performance_tracing_ will be removed. A session can trace only statements which it executes; it cannot see a trace of another session.

2. The information about traced statements will be displayed directly in cli to make it easier to see.

3. Each step of executing the statement will be captured and displayed.  The _"__Tracing a__ctivities"_ table contains each step's information about traced statements. The table has these columns: 
 * Timestamp
 * Activity
 * Elapsed Time

 

*A typical usage:*

Turn on tracing:

 
{code:java}
IoTDB> tracing on
Msg: The statement is executed successfully.
{code}
Execute statement. The information about traced statements is captured and displayed.

 
{code:java}
IoTDB> select s1 from root.sg1.d1{code}
Turn off tracing:
{code:java}
IoTDB> tracing off
Msg: The statement is executed successfully.{code}

description:
Change the fill syntax from:
{code:java}
Fill (typeClause (, typeClause)*){code}
to:
{code:java}
Fill (linearClause | previousClause | specificValueClause | previousUntilLastClause | oldTypeClause (COMMA oldTypeClause)*){code}
The new syntax is compatible with the old syntax. But only one fill method can be specified in one SQL. In order to unify the extra query time range within the query and reduce the difficulty of working with the result dataset.

