description:
I try to use like and regexp expression in 0.14.0, but I get confused in the clause of backslashes, since the like and regexp has different backslashes number.

I try the following clauses in IoTDB-client:

create timeseries root.sg1.d1.code with datatype=TEXT, encoding=plain;

insert into root.sg1.d1(timestamp, code) values(1, '\');

select code from root.sg1.d1 where code='\';

select code from root.sg1.d1 where code regexp '^\\\\$';

select code from root.sg1.d1 where code like '\\\\\\\\';

The result is:

!image-2022-08-31-16-11-25-008.png|width=822,height=909!

So it seems that 1 backslash in string means real 1 backslash character in insert and select clause with equal; but 4 backslashes means 1 backslash character in regexp clause; 8 backslashes mean 1 backslash character in like clause. Why there is the difference?(I guess the regexp use the java.util.regex.Pattern directly, and like clause directly transfer to pattern, so it need 2 times of backslashes).

But in 0.14.0's grammar, the 1 backslash in input mean 1 new backslash, this will cause we can't input character like \n as String, so there is no need to transfer them.

Will it be confused?  I think at least the num of backslashes should be mentioned in the documents. Thanks.

Update at 2022/9/3:

It seems that the like clause will regard 2 backslashes and 4 backslashes as the same (after 4 repeated backslashes)

!image-2022-09-03-10-36-19-517.png|width=430,height=267!

summary_description_acceptance:
